  <sys:String x:Key="dlg.dangerTitle">Dangerous Action</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="dlg.dangerBody">This change can break the system or weaken security. Check the box, then confirm. If you are not sure, click Cancel.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="dlg.dangerCheck">I understand the risks and am doing this on purpose</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="dlg.previewTitle">Preview Changes</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="dlg.blocked">This item is intentionally locked. Turning it off could break Windows; WinCleaner will not allow it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.diagtrack.name">Connected User Experiences (DiagTrack)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.diagtrack.desc">What it does: One of Windows’s main services that sends usage data to Microsoft.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Less background reporting; most people have no issues.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Gamer note: not an FPS miracle, but better privacy and less background work.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dmwappush.name">Device Management WAP Push</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dmwappush.desc">What it does: A background service for old-style push / device management messages and related telemetry.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Most home users notice nothing different.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Safe to disable.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wer.name">Windows Error Reporting</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wer.desc">What it does: Sends crash reports to Microsoft when a program fails.&#x0a;If you turn it off / set to Manual: Reports stop or only run when needed. It does not prevent crashes.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Manual is usually enough.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xblauth.name">Xbox Live Auth Manager</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xblauth.desc">What it does: Sign-in for Xbox / Microsoft account games and Game Pass.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Xbox app, some PC Game Pass games, and friends list may break.&#x0a;When to disable: Only if you never use Xbox or Game Pass. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xblgame.name">Xbox Live Game Save</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xblgame.desc">What it does: Cloud save sync on the Xbox side.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Cloud save sync may stop; local saves still work.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Be careful if you use Game Pass.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xboxnet.name">Xbox Live Networking</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xboxnet.desc">What it does: Network connection for Xbox online features.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Multiplayer and Xbox social features may be affected.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xboxgip.name">Xbox Accessory Management</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xboxgip.desc">What it does: Manages Xbox controllers and accessories.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Wireless Xbox controllers may behave oddly. Do not touch this if you use a controller.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.spooler.name">Print Spooler</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.spooler.desc">What it does: The Windows service that sends jobs to your printer. If you have no printer, it sits idle for no reason.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Printing and PDF printing will not work.&#x0a;When to disable: If you never print at home. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.bthserv.name">Bluetooth Support</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.bthserv.desc">What it does: Powers Bluetooth devices like headphones, mice, and controllers.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Bluetooth stops working entirely.&#x0a;When to disable: Only if you never use Bluetooth. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.onesync.name">Sync Host (OneSync)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.onesync.desc">What it does: Syncs accounts for Mail, Calendar, Contacts, and similar apps.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Built-in Mail/Calendar sync may break.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.cdp.name">Connected Devices Platform</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.cdp.desc">What it does: Features like Nearby sharing and phone–PC bridge.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Those features stop working; normal gaming is unaffected.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.diagsvc.name">Diagnostic Execution Service</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.diagsvc.desc">What it does: Runs Windows troubleshooting jobs in the background.&#x0a;Setting to Manual: Runs only when needed; less daily load.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dusm.name">Data Usage</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dusm.desc">What it does: Feeds the data usage counter in Settings.&#x0a;If you turn it off: The counter may break; your internet will not be cut off.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.sysmain.name">SysMain (Superfetch)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.sysmain.desc">What it does: Windows tries to predict and preload apps into memory. On SSDs it can sometimes waste disk activity for little gain.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Many gamers report less 100% disk usage; some app launches may be a few milliseconds slower.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Often disabled on SSD gaming PCs.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wsearch.name">Windows Search</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wsearch.desc">What it does: Keeps a search index (catalog) of your files and pokes the disk in the background.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Start menu search gets slower, but search still works. Your disk gets a break.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.rpcss.name">Remote Procedure Call (RPC)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.rpcss.desc">What it does: Windows’s internal communication layer; almost everything depends on it.&#x0a;If you turn it off: The system breaks or will not boot.&#x0a;WinCleaner locks this on purpose. Do not touch it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dcom.name">DCOM Server Process Launcher</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dcom.desc">What it does: A core piece that lets programs call each other.&#x0a;If you turn it off: You will break Windows.&#x0a;Locked. Do not touch it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.windefend.name">Windows Defender Antivirus</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.windefend.desc">What it does: Windows’s built-in antivirus.&#x0a;If you turn it off: You open the door to viruses.&#x0a;WinCleaner blocks this on purpose. Do not disable it here even if you install another antivirus.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wuauserv.name">Windows Update</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wuauserv.desc">What it does: Downloads and installs security patches and updates.&#x0a;If you turn it off: No more updates; you get peace for a while but security holes pile up.&#x0a;Risky. Only if you know what you are doing. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.bits.name">Background Intelligent Transfer</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.bits.desc">What it does: Background file downloads for Windows Update and some other programs.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Updates and some downloads break.&#x0a;Risky. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.pcasvc.name">Program Compatibility Assistant (PcaSvc)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.pcasvc.desc">What it does: Watches for program compatibility issues in the background and reports them to Microsoft.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Compatibility prompts may stop; apps still run normally.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.lfsvc.name">Geolocation Service (lfsvc)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.lfsvc.desc">What it does: Provides location data to apps (pairs with the Deny Location tweak).&#x0a;If you turn it off: Maps / weather and other location features stop working.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.remoteregistry.name">Remote Registry</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.remoteregistry.desc">What it does: Lets other PCs on the network access this machine’s registry remotely.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Home users notice nothing; keeping it off is better for security.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.ssdpsrv.name">SSDP Discovery</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.ssdpsrv.desc">What it does: Automatically discovers UPnP devices on the network (smart TVs, printers, etc.).&#x0a;If you turn it off: Device discovery may need to be manual; internet itself is unaffected.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.upnphost.name">UPnP Device Host</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.upnphost.desc">What it does: Hosts UPnP devices together with SSDPSRV.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Some network device sharing may stop working.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.retaildemo.name">Retail Demo</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.retaildemo.desc">What it does: Microsoft storefront / demo-mode service; useless on a home PC.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Nothing noticeable.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Safe to disable.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wmpnetwork.name">Windows Media Player Network Sharing</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wmpnetwork.desc">What it does: Shares Windows Media Player content to devices on the home network (DLNA).&#x0a;If you turn it off: WMP network sharing stops; local playback is fine.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.tabletinput.name">Touch Keyboard and Handwriting</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.tabletinput.desc">What it does: Touch / pen keyboard and handwriting recognition.&#x0a;If you turn it off: No effect on desktops/laptops without a touch screen.&#x0a;When to disable: If you never use tablet / touch mode. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.3dviewer.name">3D Viewer</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.3dviewer.desc">What it is: An app for viewing 3D model files.&#x0a;If you remove it: You cannot open 3D files (most people never do anyway).&#x0a;How to get it back: Microsoft Store. Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.mixedreality.name">Mixed Reality Portal</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.mixedreality.desc">What it is: Setup portal for VR / mixed reality headsets.&#x0a;If you do not have VR: It is just wasted space.&#x0a;Safe to remove. Can be reinstalled from the Store.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxapp.name">Xbox app</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxapp.desc">What it is: The Xbox / Game Pass app. Windows 11 ships Microsoft.GamingApp; older PCs have Microsoft.XboxApp. WinCleaner removes whichever is installed.&#x0a;If you remove it: Game Pass, friends, and some Xbox social features may be affected.&#x0a;Think twice if you use Game Pass.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxoverlay.name">Xbox Game Bar</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxoverlay.desc">What it is: The Win+G overlay in games — recording, widgets, FPS counter.&#x0a;If you remove / disable it: Less background recording load → some PCs gain FPS.&#x0a;Popular choice for gamers. Can be reinstalled from the Store.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxidentity.name">Xbox Identity Provider</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxidentity.desc">What it is: Identity provider for your Xbox account.&#x0a;If you remove it: Xbox sign-ins may break.&#x0a;Do not remove if you use Xbox.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.skype.name">Skype</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.skype.desc">What it is: The classic video chat app.&#x0a;If you use Discord or Teams: You probably never need this.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.solitaire.name">Solitaire Collection</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.solitaire.desc">What it is: Microsoft’s card and casual games pack.&#x0a;If you do not play it: It just takes up space.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.weather.name">Weather</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.weather.desc">What it is: Bing Weather widget / app.&#x0a;If you check weather on your phone: You do not need it on PC.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.news.name">News</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.news.desc">What it is: Bing News / headlines app.&#x0a;If you hate news pushed at startup: Remove it.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.officehub.name">Office Hub</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.officehub.desc">What it is: A &quot;try / buy Office&quot; promo app.&#x0a;Whether you have Office or not: This hub is not required.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.people.name">People</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.people.desc">What it is: Contacts / address book app.&#x0a;If you do not keep contacts here: You can remove it.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.feedback.name">Feedback Hub</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.feedback.desc">What it is: App for sending complaints and suggestions to Microsoft.&#x0a;If you never use it: It just takes space.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.gethelp.name">Get Help</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.gethelp.desc">What it is: Microsoft’s help chat / tips app.&#x0a;If you just Google things: You do not need this.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.getstarted.name">Get Started</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.getstarted.desc">What it is: New PC tips tour.&#x0a;If you already looked once: You will never open it again.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.maps.name">Maps</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.maps.desc">What it is: Windows Maps.&#x0a;If you use Google Maps or your phone: Not needed on PC.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.zunevideo.name">Films &amp; TV</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.zunevideo.desc">What it is: Microsoft’s video player / movie store front.&#x0a;If you use VLC or similar: You do not need this.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.zunemusic.name">Media Player (Groove)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.zunemusic.desc">What it is: The old Groove / music app.&#x0a;If you use Spotify: This is unnecessary.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.clipchamp.name">Clipchamp</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.clipchamp.desc">What it is: Simple video editor bundled with Windows 11.&#x0a;If you do not edit videos: Remove it and free space.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.teams.name">Microsoft Teams</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.teams.desc">What it is: Work / school meeting app (sometimes preinstalled on Windows).&#x0a;If you do not use it: Stop it from sitting in the background — remove it.&#x0a;Do not remove if you need it for work.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.store.name">Microsoft Store</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.store.desc">What it is: The app store. Xbox, many UWP apps, and updates come from here.&#x0a;If you remove it: You put the system in a bad spot.&#x0a;That is why the checkbox is locked — you cannot remove it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.photos.name">Photos</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.photos.desc">What it is: Default photo and video viewer.&#x0a;If you use another viewer: You can remove it; otherwise leave it.&#x0a;Optional.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.calculator.name">Calculator</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.calculator.desc">What it is: The Calculator app.&#x0a;Marked system-critical in the catalog — checkbox locked so you cannot delete it by accident.&#x0a;If you really want to remove it, that would be a different path; it is blocked here.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.yourphone.name">Phone Link</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.yourphone.desc">What it is: Phone–PC link app.&#x0a;If you do not use it: Safe to remove.&#x0a;How to get it back: Microsoft Store.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.cortana.name">Cortana</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.cortana.desc">What it is: Voice assistant app (limited on modern Windows 10/11).&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.widgets.name">Widgets (Web Experience)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.widgets.desc">What it is: The app behind the taskbar Widgets panel.&#x0a;If you remove it: The Widgets icon goes away too.&#x0a;Remove carefully — some Win11 setups change taskbar behavior.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.paint3d.name">Paint 3D</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.paint3d.desc">What it is: 3D drawing app; most people prefer classic Paint.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.print3d.name">Print 3D</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.print3d.desc">What it is: 3D printing app; useless without a 3D printer.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.todo.name">Microsoft To Do</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.todo.desc">What it is: Task list app.&#x0a;If you use something else: Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.quickassist.name">Quick Assist</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.quickassist.desc">What it is: Remote support app.&#x0a;Rarely used, but handy in some support scenarios — remove carefully.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.copilot.name">Copilot</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.copilot.desc">What it is: The Windows Copilot chat app on the taskbar / Start.&#x0a;If you remove it: Copilot shortcuts disappear; you can reinstall from the Store.&#x0a;Remove carefully if you actually use Copilot.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.bingsearch.name">Bing Search</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.bingsearch.desc">What it is: Bing / search-highlights package that injects web results into Windows Search.&#x0a;If you remove it: Local search still works; Bing extras go away.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.outlook.name">Outlook (new)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.outlook.desc">What it is: The new Outlook for Windows app that replaces Mail / Calendar on many PCs.&#x0a;If you use classic Outlook or another mail client: Safe to remove.&#x0a;Do not remove if this is your daily mail app.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.stickynotes.name">Sticky Notes</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.stickynotes.desc">What it is: Microsoft Sticky Notes.&#x0a;If you do not use it: It just sits in the background.&#x0a;Safe to remove. Notes can be restored from the Store / your Microsoft account.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.powerautomate.name">Power Automate</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.powerautomate.desc">What it is: Desktop automation / flow app bundled with Windows.&#x0a;If you never built a flow: You do not need it.&#x0a;Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.devhome.name">Dev Home</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.devhome.desc">What it is: Microsoft’s developer dashboard (widgets, machine setup).&#x0a;If you are not a developer using it: Safe to remove.&#x0a;Windows 11 only.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxtcui.name">Xbox TCUI</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxtcui.desc">What it is: Xbox title-callable UI used by Game Bar / Game Pass popups.&#x0a;If you remove it: Some Xbox in-game prompts may break.&#x0a;Leave it if you keep Game Bar or Game Pass.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxspeech.name">Xbox speech overlay</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxspeech.desc">What it is: Xbox Game Bar speech-to-text overlay.&#x0a;If you do not use in-game voice captions: Safe to remove.&#x0a;Leave it if you use Xbox accessibility features.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.family.name">Microsoft Family</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.family.desc">What it is: Family Safety / parental controls companion app.&#x0a;If you do not manage child accounts on this PC: Safe to remove.&#x0a;How to get it back: Microsoft Store.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.startexperiences.name">Start experiences</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.startexperiences.desc">What it is: Start menu recommendations / suggested-app experiences on Windows 11.&#x0a;If you remove it: Some Start suggestions disappear.&#x0a;Remove carefully — Start still works, but recommendations change.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamemode.name">Enable Game Mode</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamemode.desc">What it does: Tells Windows &quot;I am gaming now — give other tasks less priority&quot;.&#x0a;When enabled: Background updates, indexing, and similar work step back; the game gets a bit more focus.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Back to normal behavior.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. No harm; recommended for gamers.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamebar.name">Disable Game Bar Capture</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamebar.desc">What it does: Turns off Xbox Game Bar’s background &quot;ready to record / capture&quot; feature.&#x0a;Why: When capture stays in a near-recording state, it can cost FPS.&#x0a;If you turn it off: Win+Alt+G instant clips and similar may break; FPS usually improves.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Highly recommended.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamedvr.name">Disable Game DVR Policy</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamedvr.desc">What it does: Uses policy to tell Windows &quot;Game DVR / background recording is off&quot;.&#x0a;Together with the previous setting: Cuts recording load more aggressively.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Enable if you do not capture clips.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hags.name">Hardware-accelerated GPU Scheduling</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hags.desc">What it does: Lets the GPU schedule frames with less reliance on the CPU (HAGS).&#x0a;Good case: Lower latency / better FPS. Bad case: Stutter on some driver + game combos.&#x0a;Important: Restart your PC after changing this.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Try it; turn off if things get worse.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.ultimateperf.name">Ultimate Performance Power Plan</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.ultimateperf.desc">What it does: Unlocks and selects Windows’s hidden &quot;maximum performance&quot; power plan. Reduces CPU/GPU power saving.&#x0a;Result: More consistent FPS; on laptops battery drains faster, PC may run hotter and fans spin more.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes (reverts to High Performance). Makes sense for desktop gamers.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.nagle.name">Disable Nagle (TcpAckFrequency)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.nagle.desc">What it does: Reduces &quot;collect small packets and send later&quot; behavior on the network (TcpAckFrequency).&#x0a;In games: Competitive online play may feel slightly lower ping/latency.&#x0a;Downside: On very rare networks throughput may dip a little.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Worth trying if you play online.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tcpnodelay.name">TCP NoDelay</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tcpnodelay.desc">What it does: Same family as Nagle — says &quot;send now, do not wait&quot; (TCPNoDelay).&#x0a;Used together with Nagle disable.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.fso.name">Disable Fullscreen Optimizations Mode</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.fso.desc">What it does: Reduces Windows treating games like borderless windows; more classic exclusive fullscreen feel.&#x0a;Benefit: Alt-tab and input may work better in some older or incompatible games.&#x0a;Not required for every game. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcss.name">MMCSS GPU Priority High</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcss.desc">What it does: Raises GPU priority in Windows’s &quot;Games&quot; task profile.&#x0a;Goal: Give the graphics card more room when a game is running.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Caution level (yellow) — fine on most PCs.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcssprio.name">MMCSS Priority High</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcssprio.desc">What it does: Raises the scheduling priority number for games.&#x0a;Meaning: &quot;This task is important — handle it first&quot;.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcsssched.name">MMCSS Scheduling Category High</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcsssched.desc">What it does: Sets the scheduling category for games to High.&#x0a;Same package as the two settings above — think of them together.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.bgapps.name">Restrict Background Apps</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.bgapps.desc">What it does: Limits Store (UWP) apps from running quietly in the background.&#x0a;Benefit: Less &quot;who is using my RAM/CPU&quot; mystery.&#x0a;Downside: Some notifications and background sync may lag.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Sensible for gamers.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mouseaccel.name">Disable Enhance Pointer Precision</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mouseaccel.desc">What it does: Turns off &quot;Enhance pointer precision&quot; (mouse acceleration).&#x0a;Why: In games you want crosshair speed proportional to distance; acceleration throws off aim.&#x0a;Desktop mouse may feel different at first — you get used to it.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Strongly recommended for FPS players.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.vbs.name">Disable VBS / Memory Integrity</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.vbs.desc">What it does: Turns off Hypervisor-enforced Code Integrity (Memory Integrity / core isolation).&#x0a;Benefit: On some PCs input latency / FPS consistency improves.&#x0a;Downside: Weakens kernel-level security — use with caution.&#x0a;Important: A reboot is required. Can you undo it: Yes (Enabled=1).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.dynamictick.name">Disable Dynamic Tick</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.dynamictick.desc">What it does: Sets disabledynamictick=yes via bcdedit, turning off dynamic tick timer behavior.&#x0a;Gamer note: May smooth DPC / frame times on some systems — not a miracle everywhere.&#x0a;Requires Administrator. Can you undo it: Yes (bcdedit /deletevalue).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hpet.name">Disable HPET (useplatformclock)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hpet.desc">What it does: Runs bcdedit /deletevalue useplatformclock — removes forced platform clock usage.&#x0a;Caution: Helps latency on some boards/CPUs, hurts others; measure before keeping it.&#x0a;Revert: bcdedit /set useplatformclock true. Requires Administrator.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.telemetry.name">Minimize Telemetry</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.telemetry.desc">What it does: Sets Windows diagnostic data sent to Microsoft to the lowest level.&#x0a;Result: Less &quot;Windows is watching me&quot; feeling; a bit less background traffic.&#x0a;On Home edition zero is not always guaranteed, but it still cuts a lot.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.adid.name">Disable Advertising ID</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.adid.desc">What it does: Turns off the ID apps use to track you for ads.&#x0a;Result: Less personalized advertising.&#x0a;Does not break games. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tailored.name">Disable Tailored Experiences</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tailored.desc">What it does: Stops tips, suggestions, and ads based on your diagnostic data.&#x0a;Result: Fewer &quot;just for you&quot; pop-ups.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.cortana.name">Disable Cortana Policy</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.cortana.desc">What it does: Disables Cortana assistant via policy (already weak on newer Windows).&#x0a;Result: Less voice assistant and related background activity.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.bing.name">Disable Bing Search in Start</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.bing.desc">What it does: Stops Start menu search from pulling web / Bing results when you type.&#x0a;Result: Only things on your PC show up; faster and cleaner.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. A very popular tweak.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.location.name">Deny Location Access</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.location.desc">What it does: Generally denies apps asking &quot;where am I?&quot;.&#x0a;Result: Maps / weather may not get location or may not work fully.&#x0a;Good for privacy. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.activity.name">Disable Activity History</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.activity.desc">What it does: Stops &quot;which app you opened when&quot; history from going to the cloud / Timeline.&#x0a;Result: Timeline and cross-device resume features weaken.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.visualperf.name">Best Performance Visual Effects</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.visualperf.desc">What it does: Tells Windows &quot;look fast, not pretty&quot; — shadows, smooth scrolling, and animations are reduced.&#x0a;Result: Desktop looks flatter but feels lighter.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.animations.name">Disable Window Animations</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.animations.desc">What it does: Turns off minimize / maximize window animations.&#x0a;Result: Windows appear instantly; easier on the eyes, saves milliseconds.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.transparency.name">Disable Transparency</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.transparency.desc">What it does: Turns off frosted glass (transparency) on taskbar and Start.&#x0a;Result: Slightly more matte look, slightly less GPU/CPU spent on eye candy.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.qos.name">Remove QoS Bandwidth Reserve</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.qos.desc">What it does: Clears Windows’s default reserve that keeps part of bandwidth &quot;just in case&quot; (often ~20%).&#x0a;Result: In theory all bandwidth is yours. Not everyone sees a big difference, but harmless to try.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tcpauto.name">TCP Autotuning Normal</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tcpauto.desc">What it does: Sets Windows TCP window autotuning to &quot;normal&quot; level (netsh).&#x0a;When to use: Weird download speeds or odd network behavior.&#x0a;Think of it as a one-shot command. There is nothing to undo.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.dnsflush.name">Flush DNS Cache</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.dnsflush.desc">What it does: Clears the PC’s cached &quot;website address = IP&quot; notes.&#x0a;When to use: Site will not load, goes to the wrong place, or odd behavior after VPN.&#x0a;Harmless one-time cleanup. Nothing to undo.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.netbios.name">Disable NetBIOS over TCP/IP</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.netbios.desc">What it does: Sets NetbiosOptions=2 (Disabled) on every NetBT interface subkey.&#x0a;Result: Less legacy NetBIOS chatter/broadcasts; usually fine on modern networks.&#x0a;Caution: Very old LAN sharing / some enterprise apps may be affected. Can you undo it: Yes (0).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.deliveryopt.name">Disable Delivery Optimization P2P</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.deliveryopt.desc">What it does: Stops Windows Update from sharing updates P2P with other PCs on the internet or LAN.&#x0a;Result: Your upload is not wasted; updates still come from Microsoft.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Good if you have a data cap.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.wupause.name">Disable Windows Update Service</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.wupause.desc">What it does: Completely stops the Windows Update service.&#x0a;Result: No surprise background updates BUT no security patches either.&#x0a;Risky. OK to pause briefly before a tournament or stream, then turn it back on.&#x0a;Requires &quot;allow dangerous actions&quot; in Settings. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskceip.name">Disable CEIP Consolidator Task</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskceip.desc">What it does: Disables the Customer Experience Improvement scheduled task (usage summaries to Microsoft).&#x0a;Result: One less telemetry job.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Harmless.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskusbceip.name">Disable USB CEIP Task</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskusbceip.desc">What it does: Disables the CEIP task related to USB device usage.&#x0a;Result: Less telemetry; USB devices still work fine.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.classicmenu.name">Classic Context Menu</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.classicmenu.desc">What it does: Restores the full classic right-click menu instead of Windows 11’s compact menu.&#x0a;When on: You get the classic menu directly — no &quot;Show more options&quot; step.&#x0a;When off: The CLSID key is deleted and the Win11 default menu returns.&#x0a;Note: Explorer restarts (taskbar may briefly disappear). Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.copilot.name">Disable Windows Copilot</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.copilot.desc">What it does: Turns off Windows Copilot via policy (TurnOffWindowsCopilot).&#x0a;Result: Taskbar / Win+C Copilot entry is blocked.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes. Windows 11 only.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.recall.name">Disable Windows Recall</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.recall.desc">What it does: Disables Windows AI / Recall data analysis via policy (DisableAIDataAnalysis).&#x0a;Result: Screen-recall / AI data collection is restricted.&#x0a;Recommended for privacy. Can you undo it: Yes. Windows 11 only.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.startiris.name">Hide Start Recommendations</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.startiris.desc">What it does: Turns off Start menu &quot;recommended / Iris&quot; content.&#x0a;Result: Cleaner Start menu with less suggestion noise.&#x0a;Explorer may restart. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.widgets.name">Disable Widgets / News Feed</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.widgets.desc">What it does: Disables taskbar Widgets and the News and interests feed via policy.&#x0a;Result: Widget panel goes away; less background chatter.&#x0a;Explorer may restart. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.inking.name">Disable Inking &amp; Typing Personalization</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.inking.desc">What it does: Stops sending keyboard / handwriting usage data to Microsoft for personalization.&#x0a;Result: Typing suggestions may be more generic; privacy improves.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.feedbackfreq.name">Silence Feedback Frequency</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.feedbackfreq.desc">What it does: Stops Windows &quot;Give us feedback&quot; survey prompts.&#x0a;Result: Periodic feedback pop-ups stop.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.onlinespeech.name">Disable Online Speech Recognition</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.onlinespeech.desc">What it does: Stops sending voice data to the cloud for speech recognition.&#x0a;Result: Online speech features turn off; local features are unaffected.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.powerthrottle.name">Disable Power Throttling</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.powerthrottle.desc">What it does: Globally turns off Windows CPU throttling for background / inactive windows.&#x0a;Result: Minimized helpers (Discord, etc.) are throttled less while gaming; laptop battery life may drop.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.sysresp.name">Multimedia System Responsiveness</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.sysresp.desc">What it does: Sets the CPU reserve for background tasks (default ~20%) to zero — same family as MMCSS tweaks.&#x0a;Result: Games / multimedia get more CPU time.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.coreparking.name">Disable CPU Core Parking</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.coreparking.desc">What it does: Keeps all cores active on the current power plan (no core parking).&#x0a;Result: Less lag on sudden load spikes; higher power use.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.darkmode.name">Enable Dark Mode</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.darkmode.desc">What it does: Turns on dark theme for apps and the system UI.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes (returns to light theme).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.lockscreenads.name">Disable Lock Screen Ads / Tips</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.lockscreenads.desc">What it does: Turns off Windows Spotlight ads and tip overlays on the lock screen.&#x0a;Result: A plain, static lock screen image.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.showext.name">Show File Extensions &amp; Hidden Files</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.showext.desc">What it does: Makes file extensions (.exe, .txt, etc.) and hidden files/folders visible in Explorer.&#x0a;Result: Safer — easier to spot fake &quot;photo.jpg.exe&quot; files.&#x0a;Note: Explorer restarts. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskbarleft.name">Align Taskbar Left</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskbarleft.desc">What it does: Moves Win11 centered taskbar icons back to the classic left alignment.&#x0a;Explorer restarts. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hidechat.name">Hide Taskbar Chat Icon</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hidechat.desc">What it does: Hides the Teams / Chat icon on the taskbar.&#x0a;Explorer restarts. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.searchicon.name">Shrink Search Box to Icon</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.searchicon.desc">What it does: Shrinks the wide taskbar search box to a small icon.&#x0a;Explorer restarts. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hidetaskview.name">Hide Task View Button</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hidetaskview.desc">What it does: Hides the Task View button on the taskbar (does not uninstall anything).&#x0a;Explorer restarts. Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.explorerthispc.name">Open Explorer to This PC</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.explorerthispc.desc">What it does: File Explorer opens to This PC instead of Home.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.netthrottle.name">Reset Network Throttling Index</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.netthrottle.desc">What it does: Removes Windows’ multimedia/game network packet processing limit.&#x0a;Result: Under heavy traffic (online games) packet processing delay may drop.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.ipv6.name">Disable IPv6</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.ipv6.desc">What it does: Disables IPv6 system-wide.&#x0a;Caution: Some modern network / game services (console crossplay, some VPNs) may need IPv6.&#x0a;When to use: If you have IPv6-related connectivity issues. Can you undo it: Yes (reboot recommended).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.nicpower.name">Disable NIC Power Saving</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.nicpower.desc">What it does: Turns off &quot;Allow the computer to turn off this device&quot; on network adapters so Windows cannot sleep the link.&#x0a;Result: Occasional micro-disconnects / lag spikes in online games may improve.&#x0a;Can you undo it: Yes.</sys:String>
